UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Recovery: Sleep Is the Dose, Light Is the Timer$v$, subtitle = $v$The order of this shelf, and why it is that order$v$, summary = $v$Recovery has a hierarchy: how long you sleep, when your clock is set, what you keep out of the night, and only then the extras. This shelf walks it from the top.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$One order of operations for every recovery decision$v$, $v$Tells you which lever to pull first and which to ignore$v$, $v$Sets up the six lessons that follow$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Suspected sleep apnoea or severe insomnia needs a clinician, not a hierarchy$v$, $v$Shift work breaks the assumptions here; the wake-time rule still helps, the light timing needs a specialist$v$], body_md = $v$**The hierarchy**
Recovery is not a list of tools. It is a hierarchy, and the order matters more than any single item on it. Sleep duration is the dose. Morning light is the timer that decides when the dose lands. The caffeine cut-off and a cool, dark room protect the dose once it starts. Cold, sauna and heart-rate variability are the extras: useful, measurable, and no substitute for the first three. There is no point perfecting a cold plunge on six hours of sleep.

**Why light is on a sleep shelf**
Tonight's sleep is set this morning. Light through the eyes in the first hour of the day advances the body clock, and the clock decides when melatonin rises in the evening. The most useful thing you can do for tonight is done at breakfast.

**What this shelf skips**
Sleep trackers are useful and not essential; the one trend worth reading is the last lesson. Magnesium and melatonin have small effects in specific cases and none of the leverage of a fixed wake time. A mattress matters until it is adequate, then it stops mattering.

**The usual mistake**
Treating recovery as a set of tricks. Red light, supplements and gadgets are interesting, and none of them fixes a six-hour average. Fix the wake time, get the light, cut the caffeine. Then the extras have something to add to.

**How to read this shelf**
Seven lessons in order. Each ends with a practice you can do the same day. The practice is what counts, not the reading.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Most people work on the wrong layer. Supplements and gadgets get the attention while the dose and the timer leak. Once the hierarchy is clear, every decision about recovery gets easier.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Write down your sleep dose for the last seven nights, honestly: hours in bed, not hours you meant to.$v$, $v$Pick one wake time for the next seven days, weekends included, and set the alarm now.$v$, $v$Read the next lesson tomorrow, after the first fixed wake-up.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Sleep is the dose, light is the timer, the cut-off protects the dose.$v$, $v$Extras are extras; nothing on this shelf compensates for short sleep.$v$, $v$Fix the wake time first. Bedtime follows it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What sits at the top of the recovery hierarchy?", "choices": ["How long you sleep", "A sleep tracker", "A cold plunge"], "correct": 0, "explain": "No tool on this shelf compensates for short sleep. Duration first, then timing, then protection."}, {"q": "When is tonight's sleep set?", "choices": ["At 22:00 tonight", "In the first hour after waking, by light", "At dinner"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Morning light advances the clock, and the clock decides when melatonin rises in the evening."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Watson, N. F. et al.", "title": "Recommended amount of sleep for a healthy adult: a joint consensus statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine and Sleep Research Society (Sleep)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Khalsa, S. B. S., Jewett, M. E., Cajochen, C. & Czeisler, C. A.", "title": "A phase response curve to single bright light pulses in human subjects (J. Physiol.)", "year": 2003}, {"author": "Walker, M.", "title": "Why We Sleep", "year": 2017}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which layer of the hierarchy have you been working on, and which one is actually leaking?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You held one wake time for a week. What changed in the evenings without you touching them?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 1, display_order = 1, course_role = $v$foundations$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'foundations-recovery-and-sleep';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Sleep: Seven to Nine Hours, One Wake Time$v$, subtitle = $v$The closest thing to a master lever$v$, summary = $v$Adults need 7–9 h in bed with a wake time that does not move. Below seven hours the costs arrive within a week and stay invisible to the person paying them.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "7–9 h in bed", "frequency": "Every night, weekends included", "intensity": "Wake time fixed within 30 minutes", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Memory consolidation and learning$v$, $v$Insulin sensitivity and appetite hormones$v$, $v$Immune response and mood$v$, $v$Recovery between hard sessions$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Sleep need is individual; measure your own response, not the average$v$, $v$Shift work breaks the fixed wake time; keep it fixed within each rotation$v$, $v$Chronic insomnia responds to cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia, not to willpower; ask for it$v$], body_md = $v$**The dose**
The American Academy of Sleep Medicine and the Sleep Research Society jointly recommend at least seven hours a night for adults (Watson et al. 2015). Nine is fine. Under six and over ten, night after night, is where outcomes worsen. The target is 7–9 h in bed, and the bed is the unit that matters: time asleep runs about 85 to 90% of time in bed, so a seven-hour night needs closer to eight in bed.

**What short sleep costs**
Van Dongen et al. (2003) restricted healthy adults to six hours a night for two weeks. By the end, their attention and reaction times had fallen to the level of people kept awake for two nights straight, and they rated themselves only slightly sleepy. The deficit was real and the awareness of it was gone. One week of five-hour nights lowered insulin sensitivity by roughly a fifth in healthy young men (Buxton et al. 2010) and cut testosterone by 10 to 15% (Leproult and Van Cauter 2011). People sleeping under seven hours were nearly three times as likely to catch a cold after exposure to the virus as those sleeping eight or more (Cohen et al. 2009).

**Timing beats totals**
The wake time is the lever. A fixed wake time anchors the clock, and bedtime follows within a few weeks because sleep pressure builds on schedule. A weekend shift of two hours is a small jet lag every week, which Wittmann et al. (2006) named social jet lag; it tracks with lower mood and heavier stimulant use. Sleeping in on Sunday repays some of the debt, and not the metabolic part.

**The room**
Dark enough that you cannot see your hand. Bedroom at 17–19 °C, because the core has to cool to fall asleep. Quiet, or earplugs. The phone charges in another room, since the problem is not the light, it is the reason to look.

**The order of operations**
Wake time first. Then the light lesson, then the caffeine lesson. Then, and only then, the room. Most people start with the mattress.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Sleep debt is invisible to the person carrying it. The trial subjects at six hours felt fine and performed like people who had not slept. Protect the window or pay everywhere else.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Pick a wake time you can keep seven days a week. Count back eight and a half hours: that is lights out.$v$, $v$Tonight, defend lights out as if it were a meeting with someone you respect.$v$, $v$Log hours in bed for seven nights. Anything under seven is a tax on the next day.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Seven to nine hours in bed, with a fixed wake time, is the dose.$v$, $v$Under seven hours the costs are measurable within a week and invisible to you.$v$, $v$Wake time is the lever; bedtime follows it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "After two weeks of six-hour nights, the trial subjects…", "choices": ["Slept longer at weekends and recovered", "Adapted fully", "Performed like people awake for two nights straight, and felt only slightly sleepy"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Van Dongen's 2003 finding: the deficit accumulated and the awareness of it did not."}, {"q": "Which single change anchors the sleep schedule?", "choices": ["A fixed wake time", "A new mattress", "A sleep tracker"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The clock sets to the wake time; bedtime follows once sleep pressure builds on schedule."}, {"q": "One week of five-hour nights in healthy young men…", "choices": ["Changed nothing measurable", "Lowered insulin sensitivity and testosterone", "Raised growth hormone"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Buxton 2010 and Leproult 2011: roughly a fifth off insulin sensitivity, 10 to 15% off testosterone."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Watson, N. F. et al.", "title": "Recommended amount of sleep for a healthy adult: a joint consensus statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine and Sleep Research Society (Sleep)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Van Dongen, H. P. A., Maislin, G., Mullington, J. M. & Dinges, D. F.", "title": "The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness: dose-response effects on neurobehavioral functions and sleep physiology from chronic sleep restriction and total sleep deprivation (Sleep)", "year": 2003}, {"author": "Buxton, O. M. et al.", "title": "Sleep restriction for 1 week reduces insulin sensitivity in healthy men (Diabetes)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Leproult, R. & Van Cauter, E.", "title": "Effect of 1 week of sleep restriction on testosterone levels in young healthy men (JAMA)", "year": 2011}, {"author": "Cohen, S., Doyle, W. J., Alper, C. M., Janicki-Deverts, D. & Turner, R. B.", "title": "Sleep habits and susceptibility to the common cold (Arch. Intern. Med.)", "year": 2009}, {"author": "Wittmann, M., Dinich, J., Merrow, M. & Roenneberg, T.", "title": "Social jetlag: misalignment of biological and social time (Chronobiol. Int.)", "year": 2006}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is your real wake time this week, and what does it cost you to hold it on Saturday?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week at one wake time. Did you fall asleep earlier without trying, or is something else keeping you up?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 2, display_order = 2, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'sleep-7-9-hours';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Morning Light: The Timer for Tonight$v$, subtitle = $v$Ten minutes outside sets the clock that decides when you get sleepy$v$, summary = $v$Bright light through the eyes within 60 minutes of waking advances the body clock, so melatonin rises earlier that evening. Five to ten minutes under a clear sky, longer under cloud.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5–10 min clear sky · 15–20 min overcast", "frequency": "Daily, within 60 minutes of waking", "intensity": "Outdoor light, eyes open, no sunglasses", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Earlier melatonin rise and easier sleep onset that night$v$, $v$Sharper morning alertness$v$, $v$Lower depression odds across large cohorts$v$, $v$Steadier energy through the day$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Never look at the sun; looking around is enough$v$, $v$At high UV, get the light early and keep the exposure short$v$, $v$Bright lamps for winter use: anyone with bipolar disorder or a retinal condition asks a clinician first$v$], body_md = $v$**The cells that do it**
The retina holds a small population of ganglion cells that respond to light directly, without the rods and cones, and report to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the clock behind the eyes (Berson et al. 2002). They respond to bright, blue-rich light and they need photons through the open eye. Skin does not count. Sunglasses block them, and a window cuts the signal roughly in half.

**What it does today**
Light in the first hour advances the clock. Khalsa et al. (2003) mapped the human response curve: a bright pulse after the body's temperature minimum, which is early morning for most people, shifts the clock earlier, and the same pulse late at night shifts it later. Earlier clock, earlier melatonin, easier sleep onset that night. The morning cortisol rise sharpens with it, which is the clear-headed feeling a dark morning never gives you.

**What it does over years**
Burns et al. (2021) looked at time spent in outdoor light across more than 400,000 adults in the UK Biobank. Each extra hour outdoors in daylight tracked with lower odds of lifetime depression, less antidepressant use, better sleep and an earlier chronotype. It is an association, and it is a large and consistent one.

**The dose**
The numbers are about brightness. A bright office is 300 to 500 lux; an overcast sky gives several thousand; a clear morning gives tens of thousands. So the dose scales with the weather: 5–10 minutes under a clear sky, 15–20 minutes under cloud, within 60 minutes of waking, no sunglasses, eyes open and looking around, never at the sun. Before dawn in a Finnish winter, a 10,000-lux lamp at desk distance for 20–30 minutes does most of the job until the sun is up.

**Make it automatic**
Attach it to something that already happens: the first coffee on the step, the dog, the walk to the tram. A thing that needs deciding every morning will lose to the phone.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Indoor light looks bright and is a hundred times dimmer than a cloudy sky. Without a strong morning signal the clock drifts later each day, and the evening pays. The fix is free and takes ten minutes.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Tomorrow, be outside within 60 minutes of waking, eyes open, no sunglasses.$v$, $v$Stay 5–10 minutes if the sky is clear, 15–20 if it is overcast.$v$, $v$Attach it to the first coffee or the first walk so it stops being a decision.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Light through the eyes within 60 minutes of waking sets tonight's melatonin.$v$, $v$The dose scales with brightness: 5–10 minutes clear, 15–20 minutes overcast.$v$, $v$Indoor light does not count, and sunglasses cancel it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Why does a bright office not count as morning light?", "choices": ["Glass blocks all light", "It counts", "It is 10 to 100 times dimmer than daylight, below the clock's threshold"], "correct": 2, "explain": "300 to 500 lux indoors against several thousand under cloud and tens of thousands in sun."}, {"q": "What does morning light do to the body clock?", "choices": ["Advances it, so melatonin rises earlier that evening", "Nothing measurable", "Delays it"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Khalsa's 2003 response curve: light after the temperature minimum shifts the clock earlier."}, {"q": "Under an overcast sky, how long?", "choices": ["1 minute", "15–20 minutes", "2 hours"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Dimmer light, longer dose. Clear sky needs 5–10 minutes."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Berson, D. M., Dunn, F. A. & Takao, M.", "title": "Phototransduction by retinal ganglion cells that set the circadian clock (Science)", "year": 2002}, {"author": "Khalsa, S. B. S., Jewett, M. E., Cajochen, C. & Czeisler, C. A.", "title": "A phase response curve to single bright light pulses in human subjects (J. Physiol.)", "year": 2003}, {"author": "Burns, A. C. et al.", "title": "Time spent in outdoor light is associated with mood, sleep, and circadian rhythm-related outcomes: a cross-sectional and longitudinal study in over 400,000 UK Biobank participants (J. Affect. Disord.)", "year": 2021}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Where are your eyes in the first hour of the day, and how much sky do they see?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of morning light. Did sleepiness arrive earlier in the evening, and by how much?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 3, display_order = 3, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'morning-light-anchor';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$The Caffeine Cut-Off$v$, subtitle = $v$Why the 16:00 coffee costs you deep sleep you never notice losing$v$, summary = $v$Caffeine has a half-life of 5–7 h, so an afternoon coffee is still half active at bedtime. The last dose goes 8–10 h before bed, which for most people means early afternoon.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "Daily", "frequency": "Last caffeine 8–10 h before bed", "intensity": "All sources: coffee, tea, pre-workout, cola, chocolate", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$More deep sleep within a week$v$, $v$Faster sleep onset$v$, $v$Morning energy that does not depend on the first cup$v$, $v$Caffeine that works again when you need it$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Two to four days of headache and fatigue as adenosine signalling recalibrates$v$, $v$Slow metabolisers, pregnancy and the pill lengthen the half-life; move the cut-off earlier$v$, $v$Decaf is not zero; it is fine, and it counts$v$], body_md = $v$**The chemistry**
Adenosine builds up in the brain across the day and is the pressure you feel as tiredness. Caffeine sits in adenosine's receptors and blocks the signal. The pressure is still there; you stop reading it. When the caffeine clears, it lands all at once.

The half-life is 5–7 h in most adults (Clark and Landolt 2017), longer in slow metabolisers, in pregnancy and on oral contraceptives. A 200 mg coffee at 16:00 leaves about 100 mg at 22:00 and 50 mg at 04:00.

**What it does to sleep you thought was fine**
Drake et al. (2013) gave 400 mg of caffeine at bedtime, three hours before, and six hours before, against a placebo, and recorded sleep in the lab. Even the six-hour dose cut total sleep by more than an hour and disturbed its architecture. The subjects' own ratings barely moved. You can fall asleep on time with caffeine on board and still lose the deep sleep in which growth hormone is released and the brain clears the day's waste. Falling asleep is not the test.

**The rule**
Last caffeine 8–10 h before bed. Bed at 23:00 means the last cup between 13:00 and 15:00; bed at 22:00 means between 12:00 and 14:00. Count everything: pre-workout, green tea, matcha, cola, dark chocolate, most energy drinks. Decaf carries 5 to 10 mg, which is fine and still counts.

**If the afternoon needs it**
An afternoon that cannot run without coffee is usually a sleep debt or a lunch that was mostly refined carbohydrate. Ten minutes of walking or a protein snack handles the dip. Ten minutes lying down with the eyes closed handles the rest.

**The two-week test**
Cut caffeine at the rule for fourteen days. Most people report faster sleep onset within a week and steadier morning energy within two. If nothing changes, go back; the test cost nothing.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You can sleep on caffeine and still get poor sleep. The loss is in the deep stages, which you cannot feel and cannot report. Two weeks without the afternoon cup is the only way to find out what it was costing.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Work out your cut-off: bedtime minus eight to ten hours. Write it on the kettle.$v$, $v$Replace the afternoon cup today with water, a walk or ten minutes lying down with the eyes closed.$v$, $v$Run it for fourteen days before judging.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Half-life 5–7 h: the 16:00 coffee is half active at bedtime.$v$, $v$Last dose 8–10 h before bed, and everything with caffeine in it counts.$v$, $v$Falling asleep on time proves nothing; the loss is in the deep stages.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Caffeine six hours before bed, in the lab…", "choices": ["Improved deep sleep", "Had no measurable effect", "Cut total sleep by more than an hour while subjects barely noticed"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Drake 2013: the six-hour dose still cost over an hour of sleep, and the subjective ratings barely moved."}, {"q": "For a 23:00 bedtime, the last caffeine goes at…", "choices": ["Between 13:00 and 15:00", "Any time; it does not matter", "20:00"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Eight to ten hours before bed. Count tea, cola and chocolate too."}, {"q": "What does caffeine do to adenosine?", "choices": ["Removes it", "Blocks its receptors, so the pressure is hidden, not gone", "Doubles it"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The tiredness is still building; you stop reading it until the caffeine clears."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Drake, C., Roehrs, T., Shambroom, J. & Roth, T.", "title": "Caffeine effects on sleep taken 0, 3, or 6 hours before going to bed (J. Clin. Sleep Med.)", "year": 2013}, {"author": "Clark, I. & Landolt, H. P.", "title": "Coffee, caffeine, and sleep: a systematic review of epidemiological studies and randomized controlled trials (Sleep Med. Rev.)", "year": 2017}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$When was your last caffeine yesterday, and how did the night after it go?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Two weeks at the cut-off. What changed first: falling asleep, the night, or the morning?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 4, display_order = 4, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'caffeine-cutoff';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Cold Exposure: Dose, Timing and Who Should Skip It$v$, subtitle = $v$Two to four short immersions a week, never after a strength session$v$, summary = $v$Cold water gives hours of alertness and a mood lift, and the same signal blunts muscle growth when it follows a strength session. The dose is small: 1–3 minutes at 15 °C or below, 2–4 times a week.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "1–3 min per immersion", "frequency": "2–4 times a week, at least 6 h from strength work, not within 4 h of bed", "intensity": "Water at 15 °C or below", "prerequisites": "Healthy heart and blood pressure; not pregnant; no Raynaud's"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Hours of alertness without a crash$v$, $v$A daily practice of doing a hard thing on purpose$v$, $v$Possible gains in insulin sensitivity and brown fat, still early$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Blunts muscle growth when taken within six hours after lifting$v$, $v$Too activating late in the evening$v$, $v$Heart conditions, uncontrolled blood pressure, Raynaud's, pregnancy: clinician first$v$, $v$Never alone in open water; the cold shock response is what drowns people$v$], body_md = $v$**The dose**
The number most people quote comes from Søberg et al. (2021), who studied Danish winter swimmers: the group's routine added up to about 11 minutes a week of cold, split across two to four short immersions and alternated with sauna. The study was about brown fat and heat production, not mood, and the swimmers were experienced. Treat the 11 minutes as a ceiling to build toward, not a target for week one. In practice: 2–4 sessions a week, 1–3 minutes each, water at 15 °C or colder. At that temperature the body cannot pretend; you are cold whether or not you breathe well through it.

**Why it feels the way it does**
Šrámek et al. (2000) immersed volunteers in 14 °C water for an hour and measured a rise in noradrenaline of about 530% and in dopamine of about 250% above baseline. That is an hour in the water, far more than anyone here will do, so do not expect the same numbers from a three-minute shower. The direction holds: a short immersion produces a clean, stimulant-free alertness that lasts for hours and does not crash. The evidence for mood beyond that is small and early, mostly open-water swimming studies without controls, which is why the chip says promising.

**When not to**
Within six hours after a strength or muscle-building session. Roberts et al. (2015) and Fyfe et al. (2019) both found that cold immersion after lifting suppressed the anabolic signalling and reduced long-term muscle growth compared with an easy cool-down; strength gains were less affected. The inflammation you are cooling is the adaptation. If muscle is the goal, cold goes on rest days, in the morning, or before training. Not after.

**How to start**
End the morning shower with 30 seconds of cold and add 15 seconds a week. Breathe out long through the first gasp; the gasp is a reflex and it passes in about half a minute. Stop at three minutes; past that the risk rises and the return does not. Warm up with movement and dry clothes rather than a hot shower, because the rewarming is part of the response.

**Who skips it**
Anyone with a heart condition, uncontrolled blood pressure, Raynaud's or a pregnancy talks to a clinician first. The cold shock response, a sharp gasp and a spike in heart rate, is what makes open water dangerous for the unprepared (Tipton et al. 2017). Never alone in open water, and never into cold after alcohol.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The alertness is real, and it is the same signal that blocks muscle growth after a session. Cold is a timing question before it is a dose question.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$End today's shower with 30 seconds of cold. Cool, not brutal.$v$, $v$Add 15 seconds a week until you are at 1–3 minutes.$v$, $v$Put cold on the calendar away from strength sessions: morning if you lift in the evening, rest days if you lift in the morning.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$1–3 minutes at 15 °C or below, 2–4 times a week; about 11 minutes a week of cold is the ceiling, not the starting point.$v$, $v$Hours of clean alertness; the mood evidence is small and early.$v$, $v$Never within six hours after a strength session; the inflammation is the adaptation.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "When is the worst time for cold water?", "choices": ["On a rest day", "First thing in the morning", "Within six hours after a strength session"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Roberts 2015 and Fyfe 2019: post-lifting cold suppressed anabolic signalling and long-term muscle growth."}, {"q": "The 250% dopamine figure came from…", "choices": ["An hour in 14 °C water", "A sauna", "A three-minute cold shower"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Šrámek 2000. The direction transfers to short immersions; the size of the number does not."}, {"q": "How long per immersion?", "choices": ["As long as you can stand", "1–3 minutes", "15 minutes"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Past three minutes the risk rises and the return does not, for anyone who is not acclimatised."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Søberg, S. et al.", "title": "Altered brown fat thermoregulation and enhanced cold-induced thermogenesis in young, healthy, winter-swimming men (Cell Rep. Med.)", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Šrámek, P., Šimečková, M., Janský, L., Šavlíková, J. & Vybíral, S.", "title": "Human physiological responses to immersion into water of different temperatures (Eur. J. Appl. Physiol.)", "year": 2000}, {"author": "Roberts, L. A. et al.", "title": "Post-exercise cold water immersion attenuates acute anabolic signalling and long-term adaptations in muscle to strength training (J. Physiol.)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Fyfe, J. J. et al.", "title": "Cold water immersion attenuates anabolic signaling and skeletal muscle fiber hypertrophy, but not strength gain, following whole-body resistance training (J. Appl. Physiol.)", "year": 2019}, {"author": "Tipton, M. J., Collier, N., Massey, H., Corbett, J. & Harper, M.", "title": "Cold water immersion: kill or cure? (Exp. Physiol.)", "year": 2017}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What are you hoping cold will do for you, and would a fixed wake time do it more cheaply?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$After a week of cold mornings, what did the next two hours feel like, and did any session suffer?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 5, display_order = 5, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'cold-exposure';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Sauna: The Finnish Evidence$v$, subtitle = $v$What twenty years of Kuopio data say, and what they do not$v$, summary = $v$Men who took a sauna four or more times a week had 40% lower all-cause mortality over twenty years than men who went once. The number is an association from Finland, and the routine is the ordinary one.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10–20 min per round", "frequency": "2–4 times a week", "intensity": "80–90 °C", "prerequisites": "Hydrated, sober, no acute illness"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Strong association with lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality$v$, $v$Relaxation and a measurable sleep-onset effect when timed before bed$v$, $v$Fits after training without blunting adaptation$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Hydrate; never after alcohol$v$, $v$Pregnancy, heart disease, low blood pressure: ask a clinician$v$, $v$The mortality data are observational; the habit may mark people who are healthy for other reasons$v$], body_md = $v$**The study everyone quotes**
Laukkanen et al. (2015) followed 2,315 middle-aged men in Kuopio for a median of twenty years. Compared with one sauna a week, two to three sessions tracked with 24% lower all-cause mortality and four to seven sessions with 40% lower, after adjusting for fitness, blood pressure, smoking and the rest. Sessions over nineteen minutes did better than short ones. This is a cohort, not a trial; men who sauna often may differ in ways the adjustment missed. It is also the largest and cleanest data set on a recovery habit most Finns already have.

**Why it might work**
A hot sauna raises the heart rate to the level of a brisk walk, dilates the vessels and lowers blood pressure afterward. Repeated, it looks like a mild training stimulus for the parts of the cardiovascular system that exercise trains, which is the leading explanation for the association. Heat shock proteins and better endothelial function are plausible contributors and less well shown in people.

**The routine**
80–90 °C, 10–20 minutes, then cool down properly, then repeat if you like. Two to four times a week is where the associations are strongest. Drink water before and after. Not after alcohol, which is where sauna deaths in Finland come from.

**Sauna and training**
Unlike cold, there is no good evidence that heat after a session blunts the adaptation. Sauna sits well after training or on rest days. If the next day's session feels flat, shorten the sauna, not the session.

**No sauna**
A bath at about 40 °C for ten to twenty minutes, finished one to two hours before bed, shortens the time it takes to fall asleep by roughly nine minutes in a meta-analysis of passive heating studies (Haghayegh et al. 2019). The mechanism is the drop in core temperature afterward, which is the same reason a cool room helps you sleep.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Most Finns have a sauna and treat it as a habit, not a health tool. The Kuopio data suggest the habit is one of the better ones, and the routine that shows the association is the ordinary one.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Book one sauna this week: 10–20 minutes at 80–90 °C, water before and after.$v$, $v$If it is for sleep, finish one to two hours before bed.$v$, $v$No sauna: a hot bath, ten to twenty minutes, same timing.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Two to four sessions a week, 10–20 minutes each, is where the mortality associations are strongest.$v$, $v$Cohort data, not a trial; the mechanism is plausible and the number may be inflated by who chooses to sauna.$v$, $v$A hot bath before bed captures the sleep effect.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "The 40% lower mortality figure compared…", "choices": ["Long sessions with short ones", "Sauna users with non-users", "Four to seven sessions a week with one a week"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Laukkanen 2015: the reference group was once-a-week users, since almost every Finnish man in the cohort used a sauna."}, {"q": "What kind of evidence is the Kuopio study?", "choices": ["A twenty-year cohort with statistical adjustment", "A case report", "A randomised trial"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Strong association, adjusted for the obvious confounders, and still not a trial."}, {"q": "Why does a hot bath before bed help sleep?", "choices": ["It warms you for the night", "The core temperature drops afterward, which is the signal for sleep", "It relaxes the muscles"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Haghayegh 2019: passive heating one to two hours before bed shortened sleep onset by about nine minutes."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Laukkanen, T., Khan, H., Zaccardi, F. & Laukkanen, J. A.", "title": "Association between sauna bathing and fatal cardiovascular and all-cause mortality events (JAMA Intern. Med.)", "year": 2015}, {"author": "Haghayegh, S., Khoshnevis, S., Smolensky, M. H., Diller, K. R. & Castriotta, R. J.", "title": "Before-bedtime passive body heating by warm shower or bath to improve sleep: a systematic review and meta-analysis (Sleep Med. Rev.)", "year": 2019}, {"author": "Laukkanen, J. A., Laukkanen, T. & Kunutsor, S. K.", "title": "Cardiovascular and other health benefits of sauna bathing: a review of the evidence (Mayo Clin. Proc.)", "year": 2018}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Is sauna a habit for you or a treat, and what would change if it were the other?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$After a week with two saunas, what did sleep and the day after look like?$v$, practice_minutes = 20, lesson_number = 6, display_order = 6, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'sauna-heat-exposure';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$HRV: Use the Trend, Not the Number$v$, subtitle = $v$What heart-rate variability reflects, and the two things that move it most$v$, summary = $v$Heart-rate variability is a noisy morning number and a useful weekly trend. Read it as a seven-day average, and expect sleep and alcohol to move it more than any gadget.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "1 min each morning", "frequency": "Daily, the same way every time", "intensity": "Read the 7-day average against your own baseline", "prerequisites": "A wearable or a chest strap, and a consistent morning"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$An objective recovery trend$v$, $v$A rule for hard versus easy days$v$, $v$Early warning of overreaching and illness$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Day-to-day noise is large; never act on a single reading$v$, $v$Not a medical measurement; palpitations or fainting go to a doctor, not an app$v$, $v$Watching the number can become its own stress; if it does, stop for a month$v$], body_md = $v$**What it is**
Heart-rate variability is the variation in the gaps between heartbeats, measured in milliseconds. A heart under parasympathetic control, the rest-and-digest branch, speeds up and slows down with each breath, so the gaps vary. Under stress, illness, fatigue or alcohol the sympathetic branch takes over and the rhythm flattens. Higher HRV, against your own baseline, generally means better recovery status; lower means the system is loaded.

**Why the daily number lies**
A single morning reading swings with posture, breathing, the hour, a late meal or one glass of wine. Plews et al. (2013) tracked elite endurance athletes and found that a seven-day rolling average predicted training response and overreaching where single readings did not. One low morning means nothing. Five falling mornings mean something.

**How to use it**
Measure the same way each morning, before coffee and the phone, lying or sitting as you always do. Watch the seven-day average against your own baseline, not against anyone else's number. When the average has fallen for several days, make the next session easy, protect sleep and look for the cause. When it is steady or rising, hard sessions are on. That is the whole method.

**What moves it**
Sleep and alcohol dominate. Pietilä et al. (2018) analysed a night of recordings from 4,098 Finnish employees: even a low dose of alcohol lowered HRV through the first hours of sleep, and a high dose cut it by roughly a third. Training load, illness and stress follow. The trend is a mirror for the first three lessons on this shelf; it does not replace them.

**What it is not**
A diagnosis, a competition, or a reason to skip a session you were dreading anyway. If the trend and your body disagree, listen to the body and write it down.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The number is easy to get and easy to misread. Used as a weekly trend against your own baseline it tells you when to push and when to back off, which is the one question a training week keeps asking.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Before your next hard session, check the seven-day average, not today's reading.$v$, $v$Note last night's sleep and alcohol next to it; the pattern will be obvious within two weeks.$v$, $v$If the trend has fallen for five days, make today's session easy on purpose.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Read the seven-day average against your own baseline; single readings are noise.$v$, $v$Falling trend: easy day, protect sleep. Steady or rising: go.$v$, $v$Sleep and alcohol move HRV more than anything a device sells you.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Which reading should guide a training decision?", "choices": ["A friend's number", "This morning's number", "The seven-day average against your own baseline"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Plews 2013: the rolling average predicted adaptation and overreaching; single days did not."}, {"q": "What moves HRV most?", "choices": ["Sleep and alcohol", "Stretching", "Supplements"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Pietilä 2018, 4,098 Finnish employees: even a low dose of alcohol lowered HRV in the first hours of sleep."}, {"q": "A steady or rising trend means…", "choices": ["Rest", "Hard sessions are on", "Illness"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Green light. A trend that has fallen for several days is the signal to go easy."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Plews, D. J., Laursen, P. B., Stanley, J., Kilding, A. E. & Buchheit, M.", "title": "Training adaptation and heart rate variability in elite endurance athletes: opening the door to effective monitoring (Sports Med.)", "year": 2013}, {"author": "Pietilä, J. et al.", "title": "Acute effect of alcohol intake on cardiovascular autonomic regulation during the first hours of sleep in a large real-world sample of Finnish employees: observational study (JMIR Ment. Health)", "year": 2018}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What do you actually do differently on a day your body feels flat, and would a number change that?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Two weeks of trend-watching. Which nights moved the number, and did you change anything because of it?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 7, display_order = 7, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'hrv-how-to-use-it';
